+# Feature Specification: JSON Patch
+
+**Created**: 2026-11-20 | **Status**: Draft
+**Standard**: JSON Patch, RFC 6902 — §3 (document structure), §4 (operations: add §4.1, remove §4.2, replace §4.3, move §4.4, copy §4.5, test §4.6), §5 (error handling). Paths are JSON Pointers, RFC 6901 (reused from feature 001).
+**Input**: Export `applyPatch(document, patch)` from `src/index.ts`; apply an array of operations and return the new document; never mutate input; throw when the patch is invalid or an operation fails.
+
+## User Scenarios & Testing
+
+### User Story 1 - Apply a patch (Priority: P1)
+A service author applies a patch to a document and gets the updated document back, the original unchanged.
+**Independent Test**: run the RFC 6902 Appendix A examples (A.1–A.16).
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+1. **Given** each of the six operations, **When** applied per §4, **Then** the result matches the RFC examples.
+2. **Given** a patch, **When** applied, **Then** the input document and patch are not modified and the result shares no mutable structure with them.
+3. **Given** operations in sequence, **Then** each sees the result of the previous one (§5).
+
+### User Story 2 - Reject invalid patches and failed operations (Priority: P1)
+1. **Given** a patch that is not an array, an operation that is not an object, a missing/unknown `op`, a missing or non-string `path`/`from`, or a missing `value` where required, **Then** a typed error is thrown.
+2. **Given** any operation that fails (missing target, bad index, failed `test`, invalid pointer), **Then** a typed error is thrown and nothing is returned; the input is unchanged (atomic, §5).
+
+### Edge Cases
+- `add` with `-` appends; index equal to length appends; index > length fails; leading-zero index fails (§4.1).
+- `add` to an existing object member replaces it; parent must exist.
+- `remove`/`replace` of a nonexistent target fails (§4.2, §4.3). `"value": null` is a valid value.
+- `move`: `from` must exist; `from` being a proper prefix of `path` fails (§4.4); `from == path` is a no-op.
+- `copy` deep-copies; later edits to one copy do not affect the other.
+- `test`: objects equal regardless of member order, arrays ordered, numbers by value, strings by code points, types must match (§4.6); `1` ≠ `"1"`, `null` ≠ missing.
+- Whole-document path `""`: `add`/`replace`/`test`/`copy`/`move` target replace/compare the document; `remove` of the whole document fails (no document would remain).
+- `__proto__`, `constructor`, `prototype` as keys are ordinary members and never alter prototypes.
+- Extra members in an operation are ignored (§4).
+
+## Requirements
+- **FR-001**: `patch` must be an array; each element an object with string `op` in {add, remove, replace, move, copy, test} and string `path` (§3, §4).
+- **FR-002**: `add`, `replace`, `test` require a `value` member (any JSON value including `null`); `move`, `copy` require string `from` (§4).
+- **FR-003**: Operations apply in order, each to the previous result (§5).
+- **FR-004**: Semantics of each operation exactly as §4.1–§4.6 (see Edge Cases).
+- **FR-005**: Any error throws `PatchError` (invalid pointers surface as `PointerError`); no partial result (§5).
+- **FR-006**: Inputs never mutated; result is independent of inputs.
+- **FR-007**: Own members only; prototype pollution impossible.
+- **FR-008**: Doc-commented strictly typed export from `src/index.ts`; no runtime deps; README updated.
+
+### Key Entities
+- **Operation**: `{op, path, value?, from?}`. **PatchError**: exported typed error.
+
+## Success Criteria
+- **SC-001**: All RFC 6902 Appendix A examples produce the specified results/errors.
+- **SC-002**: Every failure case above throws and leaves inputs unchanged.
+- **SC-003**: Tests pass and type-check is clean.
+
+## Assumptions
+- Removing the root throws since RFC 6902 does not define an empty result.
+- Reuses pointer parsing from feature 001 (RFC 6901).
